a common theme that I see in my comment  section of people saying that they want  to study physics but they're too bad at  maths to do so and I thought I'd share a  few thoughts on this idea one is that  you don't need to be a mathematical  genius to study physics or to be a  physicist yes maths is an important tool  for physics but it's not the most  important thing the most important thing  is wanting to understand physical  phenomena and maths is really just the  tool you're going to use to do that you  don't need to be the absolute top of  your class and that's in fact all you  need is to have those basics of maths  down so things like basic algebra and  calculus and an understanding of how we  can describe things through equations  mainly like this sort of rigorous maths  that you're going to a sort of approach  is mainly going to be used to get you  through your coursework unless you want  to specialize in theoretical or really  maths based physics after that it's  entirely possible that even if you do  really sort of hate maths and don't want  to do it for a career that once you use  maths to get through your coursework you  can specialize in an area of physics  maybe more experimental that doesn't use  so much of those math skills but be  careful in saying that you hate maths  and and one thing to get away from it or  from letting it hold you back because I  think that you probably aren't as bad at  maths as you think you are especially if  your math skills are from what's being  assist in high school it's very easy to  confuse sort of real maths which is a  way to describe the universe with  arithmetic which is adding numbers  together and sort of memorizing things  and memorizing equations and sort of  what you're going to be assessed on at  school especially in early years of  maths at school arithmetic is not used  in physics and in fact plenty of  physicists hate arithmetic I was at a  dinner the other night with a bunch of  physics PhD students and physics  professors and when it came time to  split the bill the calculator came  straight out because that's not what you  do in higher level maths and in high  level physics it's no longer about  adding numbers together it's a  understanding trends and numbers and the  way things change I've not always been  the most confident in my own max  abilities I've not always been and I'm  not still like the most fluent and  that's I always need to give myself a  reminder at the start of every semester  say about exactly how these certain  tools are are used and how they work but  as long as you know how to teach  yourself everything like that as you go  that's all you need you don't need to  memorize hundreds of techniques and ways  to solve problems and you don't need to  know every mathematical approach you  just need to be able to find the one  that you need at the time I have friends  that studied physics with me that that  agree that that would top of their class  and Max and that have picked it up as  they go along I have one close friend in  particular who told me that at high  school he was placed in the class for  people who are bad at maths and that I  only sort of taught that bare minimum to  catch up but he managed to claw his way  out of there and graduate with a physics  degree so even if you're in a similar  situation right now you just need to be  able to claw your way out of people that  don't believe in your maths skills I  think if you're at the stage where  you're considering what you should  pursue as a career or changing majors  then you're young enough to be able to  intentionally reshape your own brain to  learn that  and that's probably going to be a  journey that you have to take just go  forwards and immerse yourself in physics  this will buy as a byproduct immerse you  in mathematics in the way that it is  actually useful by doing physics you  encounter mathematics as you need it and  I think that's okay learn it as you go  and and don't worry about getting too  far ahead of yourself once you see the  context of how mathematics is useful I  think you'll learn to appreciate it more  and seeing that use may help you  understand it in a wider in a wider  context and become better at it so  practice your max practice it in context  of of the physics that you're doing and  I'm confident that you'll get better so  good luck
 

